---
import Abbr from '../components/Abbr.astro';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import { CONTACT_EMAIL, CONTACT_MAILTO } from '../lib/contact';
import { RETENTION_DAYS } from '../lib/contact-form';
import { PLAUSIBLE_ORIGIN } from '../lib/analytics';
import { formatPostDate } from '../lib/post-date';
import { PERSON_NAME } from '../lib/profiles';
import { SITE_NAME } from '../lib/site';
import { THEME_STORAGE_KEY } from '../lib/theme';
import { TEXT_SCALE_KEY } from '../lib/text-scale';
import {
  D1_RESTORE_WINDOW_DAYS,
  WORKER_LOG_RETENTION_DAYS,
} from '../lib/platform-facts';

/*
 * Every claim is asserted by tests/privacy.spec.ts, and "Visit counts" by
 * tests/analytics.spec.ts. Cloudflare's own logging is not claimed, only linked.
 */

/* Set by hand when the policy text changes; a build date would re-date it on every deploy. */
const lastUpdated = '2026-11-13';

const CLOUDFLARE_PRIVACY = 'https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/';
const CLOUDFLARE_NEL =
  'https://developers.cloudflare.com/network-error-logging/';
const NEL_REPORT_HOST = 'a.nel.cloudflare.com';
const PLAUSIBLE_HOST = new URL(PLAUSIBLE_ORIGIN).host;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy"
  description="My website sets no cookies, loads nothing from anywhere else, and counts visits with cookie-free analytics."
>
  <PageHero title="Privacy" measure="reading" slab="none">
    <p class="standfirst">
      I am {PERSON_NAME}, and I decide what this website collects. It sets no
      cookies and loads nothing from any other website. Visits are counted with
      Plausible, which does not use cookies either. When you use the contact
      form, I store what you type so I can reply. The request for each form
      submission is logged for {WORKER_LOG_RETENTION_DAYS} days, including your{' '}
      <Abbr short="IP" long="Internet Protocol" /> address (the internet address
      of your connection).
    </p>

    <p class="mt-6 text-body">
      Last updated{' '}
      <time datetime={lastUpdated}>
        {formatPostDate(new Date(lastUpdated))}
      </time>
      .
    </p>
  </PageHero>

  <section class="reading-layout band py-section">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-page">
      <div class="prose mx-auto max-w-measure">
        <h2>What My Website Does Not Do</h2>

        <ul role="list">
          <li>
            <strong>No cookies.</strong> Nothing on {SITE_NAME} sets a cookie,
            and nothing reads one.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Two browser settings.</strong> When you choose Light or Dark
            in the Theme menu, your choice is kept in <dfn>local storage</dfn>{' '}
            (a small store of settings in your browser) under the name{' '}
            <code>{THEME_STORAGE_KEY}</code>, so the next page opens the same
            way. Choosing System deletes it. When you press Drink Me or Eat Me
            in the Text Size controls, the text size is kept the same way, under
            the name <code>{TEXT_SCALE_KEY}</code>; Just Right deletes it. Both
            stay in your browser, and nothing else is stored there. The{' '}
            <dfn>analytics script</dfn>, the code that counts visits, reads one
            more setting, plausible_ignore, which is Plausible's own off switch,
            and writes nothing. Clearing this website's data in your browser
            removes both settings.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Nothing loaded from other websites.</strong> Every file a
            page loads comes from this website: the fonts, the images and the
            analytics script. There are no videos or posts embedded from other
            websites, no third-party tracking tools and no advertising. The
            website's <dfn>security policy</dfn>, a rule your browser enforces,
            blocks every other website. The one exception is {PLAUSIBLE_HOST},
            where the analytics script may send visit counts. Error reports that
            your browser sends to Cloudflare on its own are covered under
            Hosting.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>No accounts.</strong> There is nothing to sign up for and
            nothing to log in to. The contact form, and the log kept of each
            submission, are explained below.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <h2>Visit Counts</h2>

        <p>
          I count visits with <a href="https://plausible.io/">Plausible</a>,
          analytics software that uses no cookies and does not follow you from
          website to website. I host Plausible myself on a server run by
          Infomaniak in Geneva, Switzerland. The script runs from this website
          and sends what it collects to that server at {PLAUSIBLE_HOST}. Apart
          from this website itself, it is the only place a page here sends
          anything to.
        </p>

        <p>
          The stats are kept until I delete them. They are not shared with or
          sold to anyone. Nothing is stored on your device.
        </p>

        <p>For each page you open, the analytics script sends:</p>

        <ul role="list">
          <li>the page's address</li>
          <li>
            the address of the page that linked you here, if your browser shares
            it
          </li>
        </ul>

        <p>
          Both addresses are sent without anything after a ? or #. When you
          leave the page or switch away from it, the script also sends how far
          down you scrolled and how long you had the page in view.
        </p>

        <p>When you click a link or a button, it also sends:</p>

        <ul role="list">
          <li>what kind of control it was</li>
          <li>its label</li>
          <li>which part of the page it is in</li>
          <li>for a link, where it goes</li>
        </ul>

        <p>
          For an email link, the address is left out. Nothing you type is ever
          sent, including what you write in the contact form.
        </p>

        <p>
          Like any server, mine receives your IP address and your browser's
          details with each request. Plausible uses the IP address to work out
          your country, and nothing more exact. It uses your browser details to
          work out your browser, operating system and type of device. To count
          each visitor once a day, it turns both, with a random value, into an
          identifier. It deletes the random value every 24 hours. It does not
          store the IP address or the browser details themselves.
        </p>

        <p>
          The server in front of Plausible keeps no log of visits. Its error log
          leaves out IP addresses, ports and request headers. Requests to{' '}
          {PLAUSIBLE_HOST} go straight to my server and do not pass through
          Cloudflare.
        </p>

        <p>
          The stats are backed up every night to Infomaniak Swiss Backup in
          Switzerland. They are encrypted on my server before they are sent, so
          Infomaniak cannot read them. I keep 7 daily, 4 weekly and 6 monthly
          backups.
        </p>

        <p>
          If your browser sends <dfn>Do Not Track</dfn> (a privacy setting most
          browsers offer), nothing is sent at all. Blocking JavaScript for this
          website stops it too.
        </p>

        <h2>Links Away from Here</h2>

        <p>
          The footer links to my profiles on other websites, and posts may link
          elsewhere. Those are ordinary links: nothing is sent when the page
          loads, only when you follow one. Once you do, that website's own
          policy applies.
        </p>

        <h2>Hosting</h2>

        <p>
          My website is served by a <dfn>Cloudflare Worker</dfn>, a program that
          runs on Cloudflare's servers. To deliver a page, Cloudflare receives
          the request for it, including your IP address, as any web server
          would. What Cloudflare keeps, and for how long, is covered by{' '}
          <a href={CLOUDFLARE_PRIVACY}>Cloudflare's privacy policy</a>.
        </p>

        <p>
          Requests that run my website's own code are also logged to my
          Cloudflare account so I can find and fix problems. In practice, that
          means sending the contact form; loading a page in a browser is not
          logged this way. A log entry holds the request and response, which can
          include your IP address, your browser and your approximate location
          (city, country and network provider). A <dfn>trace</dfn>, a timed
          record of the same request, holds its timing, status and the address
          requested, along with your browser and device, approximate location,
          network provider and the database lookups it ran. Logs and traces are
          deleted automatically after {WORKER_LOG_RETENTION_DAYS} days and are
          not stored with your message.
        </p>

        <p>
          Cloudflare also asks your browser to report requests to my website
          that fail, a feature called Network Error Logging. Only failures are
          reported, and only by browsers that support it. Your browser sends the
          report itself, to {NEL_REPORT_HOST}, so the page's security policy
          does not apply to it. A report holds the address requested, the type
          of error and its timing. Cloudflare says it stores no IP address or
          other personal data from these reports; its{' '}
          <a href={CLOUDFLARE_NEL}>Network Error Logging documentation</a> says
          how.
        </p>

        <h2>The Contact Form</h2>

        <p>
          The form on <a href="/contact/">the contact page</a> stores the name,
          email address and message you type, so I can reply. Stored with it
          are:
        </p>

        <ul role="list">
          <li>the time it was sent</li>
          <li>the time a copy was emailed to me</li>
          <li>how many times a failed copy has been retried</li>
          <li>a random identifier that only tells one message from another</li>
        </ul>

        <p>
          No IP address, browser or page you came from is stored with it. It is
          stored in a database restricted to the European Union. The copy goes
          to my inbox, which Google hosts, so I can read and answer it.
        </p>

        <p>
          To stop the form being used for bulk submissions, I limit submissions
          from one network address to about five a minute. For an{' '}
          <Abbr short="IPv6" long="Internet Protocol version 6" /> address (the
          newer format), the limit covers its whole /64 network, the block a
          single connection is normally given. Cloudflare's{' '}
          <dfn>rate limiter</dfn>, the service that enforces it, counts
          submissions against a{' '}
          <Abbr short="SHA-256" long="Secure Hash Algorithm 256" /> hash, a
          one-way fingerprint made from the current date and your{' '}
          <Abbr short="IPv4" long="Internet Protocol version 4" /> address or
          IPv6 /64. My website writes the hash nowhere else: it is not stored
          with your message or linked to it. A hash of an address is not
          anonymous, though. The date is no secret, so anyone holding the hash
          of an IPv4 address could recover the address by hashing every possible
          one.
        </p>

        <p>
          A daily job deletes messages from the database once they are{' '}
          {RETENTION_DAYS} days old. A run that fails is not retried, so a
          message can outlast {RETENTION_DAYS} days until the next day's run
          succeeds. The database can be restored to any moment in the last{' '}
          {D1_RESTORE_WINDOW_DAYS} days, so a deleted message can still be
          brought back for {D1_RESTORE_WINDOW_DAYS} days after it is deleted. I
          keep the emailed copy like any other email, for as long as I need it
          to reply. To have both removed sooner, write to{' '}
          <a href={CONTACT_MAILTO}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a> and say so; you do not
          need to give a reason.
        </p>

        <h2>Email</h2>

        <p>
          If you write to <a href={CONTACT_MAILTO}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>, I keep
          your message so I can reply. I do not add it to any list or pass it
          on.
        </p>

        <h2>Why I Use This Data</h2>

        <p>
          Where the <Abbr short="EU" long="European Union" after="'s" /> General
          Data Protection Regulation (
          <Abbr
            short="GDPR"
            long="General Data Protection Regulation"
            after=")"
          />{' '}
          applies, the legal basis for everything on this page is my legitimate
          interest (Article 6(1)(f)): for visit counts, logs, error reports and
          the rate limit, in keeping this website secure and working, and in
          knowing roughly how it is used; for the contact form and email, in
          reading and answering the messages people send me.
        </p>

        <h2>Data Sent Outside Europe</h2>

        <p>
          Cloudflare and Google are based in the United States and may process
          data there. Both are certified under the EU-U.S. and Swiss-U.S. Data
          Privacy Frameworks, which the European Commission and the Swiss
          Federal Council recognize as giving adequate protection. The Plausible
          server is in Switzerland, and the contact form database is restricted
          to the European Union.
        </p>

        <h2>Your Rights</h2>

        <p>
          You can ask me for a copy of the personal data I hold about you, and
          ask me to correct it, delete it or stop using it. Where the GDPR
          applies, you can also object to how I use it, ask me to restrict it,
          and ask for it in a format you can take elsewhere. Write to{' '}
          <a href={CONTACT_MAILTO}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>, and I will reply within
          30 days.
        </p>

        <p>
          If you think I have handled your data wrongly, you can complain to the
          data protection authority where you live or work. In Switzerland, that
          is the Federal Data Protection and Information Commissioner (
          <Abbr
            short="FDPIC"
            long="Federal Data Protection and Information Commissioner"
            after=")."
          />
        </p>

        <h2>Questions</h2>

        <p>
          Ask me at <a href={CONTACT_MAILTO}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
